  "prompt": "Use the uploaded user photo as the ONLY identity reference for the subject.

IDENTITY PRESERVATION IS THE HIGHEST PRIORITY.

Create a highly realistic black-and-white studio portrait of the exact person from the uploaded reference image.

Preserve the person's real facial identity, gender, approximate age, ethnicity, facial proportions, skin tone translated naturally into monochrome, eyes, eyebrows, nose, lips, jawline, ears, hairstyle, hairline, hair texture, facial hair, tattoos, piercings and all other distinctive characteristics.

Do not replace, blend, beautify or reinterpret the person's identity.

The final person must be immediately recognizable as the uploaded subject.

CORE VISUAL CONCEPT:

Create a minimalist monochrome portrait in which approximately the LEFT 55–65% of the person remains completely intact and photorealistic, while the RIGHT 35–45% progressively disintegrates into digital glitch fragments and pixel particles.

IMPORTANT:

This is not a person standing beside a glitch effect.

The person's actual physical form must appear to transform and dissolve into the digital fragments.

The transition begins inside the person's right side and progressively destroys the visible structure of the hair, forehead, temple, eyebrow area, sunglasses, cheek, ear, jaw, neck and shoulder.

The digital fragmentation then spreads outward into the empty background on the right side of the composition.

The effect must clearly communicate:

REAL HUMAN → PARTIAL DIGITAL CORRUPTION → COMPLETE PIXEL DISINTEGRATION → EMPTY BACKGROUND.

PORTRAIT:

The subject stands directly facing the camera.

Head upright.

Shoulders approximately level.

Neutral, calm, serious expression.

No smile.

Natural closed lips.

Confident minimalist fashion-editorial presence.

Frame from approximately the upper chest or shoulders to slightly above the head.

The subject is centered or positioned very slightly left of center to provide additional space for the disintegration on the right.

CLOTHING:

Simple plain black crew-neck T-shirt.

Matte black fabric.

No logos.
No graphics.
No text.
No visible branding.

SUNGLASSES:

Add sleek black fashion sunglasses.

The sunglasses should have a clean contemporary shape, preferably narrow rectangular or subtle cat-eye geometry depending on what naturally suits the subject.

Deep black lenses.

Minimal reflections.

The glasses must look physically worn by the person rather than digitally pasted onto the face.

IMPORTANT:

The RIGHT portion of the sunglasses must participate in the same disintegration effect.

The intact left lens and frame remain sharp and realistic.

Moving toward the right side, portions of the frame and lens progressively fragment into rectangular digital pieces.

MONOCHROME STYLE:

Entire image strictly black and white.

No color accents.

No chromatic aberration.

No RGB glitch.

No red, blue, cyan or green pixels.

Use only:
black,
white,
dark gray,
mid gray,
light gray.

High-end monochrome editorial photography.

DIGITAL DISINTEGRATION:

The RIGHT side of the subject gradually breaks apart into hundreds of digital fragments.

The fragmentation consists primarily of:

small square pixels,
tiny rectangular blocks,
horizontal rectangular strips,
thin glitch bars,
micro-pixels,
medium geometric fragments,
occasional larger rectangular blocks,
fine digital dust,
broken raster patterns,
fragmented scan-line structures.

Use a wide variety of fragment sizes.

Some particles should be extremely tiny.

Others should be approximately several millimeters or centimeters in apparent size.

A limited number of larger blocks may appear near the main transition area.

Do NOT make all pixels identical.

DISINTEGRATION STRUCTURE:

The effect must have a clear density gradient.

ZONE 1 — INTACT HUMAN:

Approximately the left 55–65% of the portrait remains completely photographic.

No glitch artifacts should contaminate the intact side.

Facial skin, hair, clothing and sunglasses remain sharp and realistic.

ZONE 2 — TRANSITION:

Beginning around the right forehead / temple / outer eye / cheek region, the human image begins breaking into digital geometry.

Skin and hair remain partially recognizable between missing areas.

Pixel blocks cut into the silhouette.

Small sections of the actual face disappear and become rectangular fragments.

This region should look complex, dense and highly detailed.

ZONE 3 — HEAVY DISINTEGRATION:

Farther toward the right, the original human structure becomes increasingly incomplete.

Parts of the head, ear, jaw, neck and shoulder are replaced by floating rectangular fragments.

Some fragments retain tiny photographic textures from the original subject.

For example:

a small block may contain hair texture,
another may contain skin texture,
another may contain part of the sunglasses,
another may contain black shirt fabric.

This makes it look as though the photograph itself is physically breaking apart.

ZONE 4 — PARTICLE FADE:

As fragments travel farther toward the right edge of the image, they become smaller, more separated and less dense.

The final particles dissolve naturally into the light background.

Do not create a hard rectangular boundary.

Do not create a straight vertical dividing line.

The transition edge must be irregular, organic and digitally corrupted.

ASYMMETRY:

The fragmentation pattern must NOT be uniform.

Different areas dissolve at different rates.

Hair may extend farther into the particle field.

Parts of the cheek may disappear earlier.

Some isolated pieces of the shoulder may survive farther toward the right.

Create an unpredictable but visually controlled digital breakup.

HAIR INTERACTION:

The right side of the hairstyle should progressively break apart.

Individual hair strands near the transition should first remain visible.

Then groups of hair become fragmented.

Then the hair mass turns into small dark rectangular particles.

Some tiny black fragments should drift upward and outward from the hair.

FACE INTERACTION:

Do NOT simply place glitch graphics over an intact face.

The right side of the actual face must be partially missing.

Fragments should replace portions of the:

forehead,
temple,
outer eyebrow,
outer sunglasses lens,
cheek,
ear,
jawline.

However, enough of the central facial structure must remain intact to preserve identity strongly.

The nose, lips and central facial features should remain predominantly recognizable.

SHOULDER AND CLOTHING:

The disintegration continues vertically downward through the right side of the neck and right shoulder.

The black shirt should progressively fragment into:

black rectangles,
gray blocks,
thin horizontal strips,
small square particles.

The effect should extend all the way toward the lower-right portion of the portrait.

Do not restrict the effect only to the face.

BACKGROUND:

Clean seamless light gray to nearly white studio background.

Very subtle natural tonal variation.

No visible room.

No objects.

No environment.

No patterns.

No scenery.

The background remains simple so the black digital fragments remain clearly visible.

LIGHTING:

Professional soft studio portrait lighting.

Large soft key light from slightly above and in front.

Subtle modeling shadows.

Natural facial volume.

Controlled highlights.

Deep but detailed blacks.

No harsh cinematic colored lighting.

No dramatic rim lighting.

No excessive contrast destroying skin texture.

The monochrome portrait should feel premium and photographic.

CAMERA:

Professional full-frame portrait camera.

Approximately 70–105mm portrait lens.

Camera directly in front of the subject.

Eye-level perspective.

Minimal perspective distortion.

Vertical portrait composition.

Sharp focus on the intact portion of the face.

Moderately shallow depth of field, but keep the entire facial plane sufficiently sharp.

PHOTOREALISM:

Extremely realistic human skin.

Visible pores.

Natural skin texture.

Fine facial hairs.

Individual eyebrow hairs.

Natural lips.

Detailed facial hair when present.

Realistic hair strands.

Realistic sunglasses materials.

Fine black fabric texture.

Natural facial asymmetry.

No excessive beauty retouching.

No plastic skin.

No CGI appearance.

GLITCH DETAIL:

The digital effect should resemble sophisticated data corruption and controlled digital decomposition rather than an explosion.

Fragments primarily move toward the RIGHT.

Some move slightly upward or downward, but the overall directional flow is from the subject toward the right side of the image.

Include occasional horizontal glitch streaks extending farther right.

Use areas of dense fragmentation mixed with empty gaps.

Some blocks should overlap visually.

Some should float independently.

Some should appear partially transparent.

Most should remain sharply geometric.

The smallest particles can resemble digital dust.

IMPORTANT DEPTH:

Fragments closest to the face should appear tightly connected to the disappearing human structure.

Fragments farther away become increasingly independent.

Create subtle depth variation:

some particles slightly in front of the portrait plane,
some aligned with it,
some slightly behind.

However, do not create exaggerated 3D debris.

The image should still feel like photographic digital corruption.

COMPOSITION:

The intact portion of the person visually anchors the left and center of the image.

The disintegration creates movement toward the right.

Leave sufficient negative space around the particle field.

The overall composition should remain elegant, minimalist and balanced.

CRITICAL EFFECT RULE:

The right side of the person MUST ACTUALLY BE MISSING where the disintegration occurs.

Do not render a complete intact person underneath the glitch.

Where pixels separate from the subject, those corresponding portions of the original head, hair, face, neck and clothing should disappear.

This physical absence is essential.

It must look like the subject is being converted into digital information.

IDENTITY PRIORITY:

The uploaded image controls ONLY the person's identity.

Preserve the uploaded person's:

face,
gender,
age appearance,
ethnicity,
hair,
facial hair,
distinctive characteristics.

The style reference controls ONLY:

black-and-white photography,
black clothing,
black sunglasses,
light background,
front-facing composition,
right-side pixel disintegration,
glitch geometry.

Do NOT copy another person's face.

