FINAL RESULT:

A hyper-realistic high-fashion black-and-white studio portrait of the exact uploaded person, photographed frontally against a clean light-gray background, wearing a plain black T-shirt and sleek black sunglasses.

Approximately the left 55–65% of the subject remains completely intact and photographic.

Starting organically around the right side of the hair, forehead, temple and outer eye, the actual person progressively breaks apart into hundreds of black, white and gray digital pixels, rectangular blocks, horizontal glitch strips and tiny raster fragments.

The right side of the sunglasses, cheek, ear, jaw, neck and shoulder progressively disappears into the fragmentation.

The fragments spread outward toward the right, becoming increasingly small and sparse until they dissolve into the empty background.

The result must look like the real human photograph itself is being digitally corrupted and physically converted into pixels — not like a glitch overlay placed on top of an intact portrait.